1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
They're pushy.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:04,000
They're screwy.

3
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:06,000
Real suckers.

4
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:10,000
But flip a switch, and they transform deserts

5
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
into destinations

6
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
and empower motorists to fill her up.

7
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Strong enough to cut through steel

8
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
or gentle enough to milk a cow.

9
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:23,000
These machines don't go with the flow.

10
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,000
They generate it.

11
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, the hidden world of pumps on Modern Marvels.

12
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The pump, in all its various incarnations,

13
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:48,020
is a relatively simple machine.

14
00:00:48,021 --> 00:00:52,360
After all, it performs just one basic function, transferring

15
00:00:52,361 --> 00:00:59,160
liquids or viscous materials from point A to point B.

16
00:00:59,161 --> 00:01:02,160
Perhaps it's this single purpose that masks its importance

17
00:01:02,161 --> 00:01:03,400
in our everyday lives.

18
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:10,000
For the pump is certainly one of mankind's most indispensable

19
00:01:10,001 --> 00:01:11,240
forms of technology.

20
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:15,480
Pumps are everywhere because there's liquid everywhere.

21
00:01:16,820 --> 00:01:18,940
We contact something that has to do with liquid

22
00:01:18,941 --> 00:01:20,240
almost everything we do.

23
00:01:24,860 --> 00:01:27,260
The most precious liquid that pumps convey

24
00:01:27,261 --> 00:01:33,200
is the very essence of life itself, water.

25
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:44,080
This is Lake Matthews, located near Los Angeles, California.

26
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:50,880
The lake holds 59 billion gallons of water.

27
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:54,580
That's enough to meet the needs of roughly 1.5 million people

28
00:01:54,581 --> 00:01:59,620
for an entire year. But the lake isn't connected

29
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:03,820
to any other natural body of water, and the annual rainfall

30
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:09,520
here is a paltry 11 inches. So how does the water get here?

31
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:12,040
This water came all the way from the Colorado River

32
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:18,540
Aqueduct, 242 miles to this facility in Riverside County.

33
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:24,580
To reach the source of the aqueduct system,

34
00:02:24,581 --> 00:02:29,380
to Arizona and another man-made body of water called Lake Havasu.

35
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:36,420
The lake was created by a massive concrete edifice that

36
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:40,620
bisects the Colorado River, Parker Dam.

37
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:48,940
Lake Havasu is approximately 700,000 acre-feet in storage.

38
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,940
An acre-foot, for example, is about a football field,

39
00:02:52,941 --> 00:02:55,280
field, one foot deep approximately.

40
00:02:55,380 --> 00:03:01,280
So it would be 700,000 of those acre feet.

41
00:03:01,380 --> 00:03:02,580
But the water would never make it

42
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:05,680
to Southern California if it weren't for a string of pump

43
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:08,080
houses that push it all the way to Lake Matthews.

44
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:15,300
The journey starts here, in the Whitsitt pumping plant,

45
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:19,100
where nine thirsty, high-powered centrifugal pumps

46
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:20,800
suck in water from Lake Havasu.

47
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:26,780
What we have in front of us is the shaft out of the pump.

48
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:28,540
We call it the intermediate shaft.

49
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:30,680
When it's installed, it would be stood up,

50
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:32,680
bolted to the motor coupling.

51
00:03:32,780 --> 00:03:35,680
The other end of the shaft would be bolted to what

52
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:39,180
is the top side of the impeller here.

53
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:42,000
An electric motor that has 10 times the horsepower

54
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:46,400
of an Indy race car energizes the pump shaft,

55
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:50,240
turning its impeller at 400 RPMs.

56
00:03:50,241 --> 00:03:53,440
But even with all that power, you can balance a nickel

57
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:55,480
on the pump casing.

58
00:03:55,580 --> 00:03:58,580
This impeller weighs approximately 7,500 pounds.

59
00:03:58,680 --> 00:03:59,980
This is the suction eye.

60
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:03,980
The water enters straight up into the suction eye of the pump.

61
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:07,120
The water discharges at the widest part of the pump,

62
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:09,900
out the discharge valve.

63
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,160
As its name implies, a centrifugal pump

64
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:16,860
operates on the principle of centrifugal force.

65
00:04:16,861 --> 00:04:20,160
The pump's spinning impeller sucks water in from the lake

66
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:22,700
and towards its center eye.

67
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:25,740
The veins of the impeller add velocity to the flow

68
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:30,100
and direct the water through a large diameter outlet pipe.

69
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,780
The immense volume of highly energized water passing

70
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:36,820
through the pump generates 125 pounds of pressure

71
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:37,920
per square inch.

72
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:45,480
125 psi is enough pressure to scale a mountain,

73
00:04:45,481 --> 00:04:48,480
which is exactly what the water has to do.

74
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:55,480
Here's how 15 million people in Southern California

75
00:04:55,481 --> 00:04:57,480
get a drink of water.

76
00:04:57,481 --> 00:05:01,480
We're at elevation 450 feet right now above sea level.

77
00:05:01,481 --> 00:05:04,480
We need to get it into the coastal basin, requiring us

78
00:05:04,481 --> 00:05:07,480
to lift the water approximately 1,600 feet

79
00:05:07,481 --> 00:05:11,480
over the terrain of the desert.

80
00:05:11,481 --> 00:05:15,480
These pumps are essential for moving water into Southern California.

81
00:05:15,481 --> 00:05:19,980
You could not do it without them.

82
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:21,560
At the Whitsitt pumping station,

83
00:05:21,660 --> 00:05:26,220
centrifugal pumps lift the water 291 feet to a forebay

84
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:29,800
at the Jean pumping station.

85
00:05:29,900 --> 00:05:32,900
From the forebay, another nine centrifugal pumps

86
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,100
suck water into the station and raise it

87
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:36,800
an additional 303 feet.

88
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:44,180
The process repeats itself at three additional pumping plants.

89
00:05:44,181 --> 00:05:47,980
Iron Mountain Station, 143 feet.

90
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:52,220
Eagle Mountain Station, 438 feet.

91
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:54,720
Finally, the Heinz pumping station

92
00:05:54,820 --> 00:05:58,920
pushes the water 441 feet and on to Lake Matthews.

93
00:06:01,740 --> 00:06:07,840
In 1913, William Mulholland built the Los Angeles aqueduct,

94
00:06:07,940 --> 00:06:10,740
providing an essential resource that allowed the city to grow.

95
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:17,980
In 1938, the Colorado Aqueduct provided water

96
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:19,180
to the rest of the region.

97
00:06:23,820 --> 00:06:26,420
Combined, they transformed a desert

98
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:28,520
into an economic and cultural goliath.

99
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:33,940
Less than half of Southern California's population

100
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:36,840
could be supported by its current rainfall and local

101
00:06:36,940 --> 00:06:38,980
supplies out of its groundwater.

102
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:41,540
And so you would have seen a very different Southern

103
00:06:41,541 --> 00:06:43,540
if there couldn't have been imported supplies

104
00:06:43,541 --> 00:06:45,540
like the Colorado River Aqueduct.

105
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:51,180
Centrifugal pumps are ideal for pumping high volumes

106
00:06:51,181 --> 00:06:53,560
of low-viscosity fluids.

107
00:06:53,561 --> 00:06:56,180
But it takes more than just one type of pump

108
00:06:56,181 --> 00:06:58,180
to move the world's liquids.

109
00:06:58,181 --> 00:07:00,400
When it comes to pumping thick liquids,

110
00:07:00,401 --> 00:07:03,900
rotary pumps reign supreme.

111
00:07:03,901 --> 00:07:06,400
And when you need ultra-high pressures,

112
00:07:06,401 --> 00:07:09,040
there are piston pumps.

113
00:07:09,041 --> 00:07:12,040
Together, these three pump types dominate the industry.

114
00:07:16,040 --> 00:07:19,280
The invention of the pump stems from mankind's basic need to

115
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:21,580
procure, store, and move water.

116
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:27,220
Thus, it's no surprise that the pump is one of civilization's

117
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:29,860
oldest inventions.

118
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:32,900
Greek philosopher and mathematician Archimedes

119
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,860
invented one of the earliest rotary pumps in 200 BC.

120
00:07:37,861 --> 00:07:40,160
Its principle is really very simple.

121
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:43,460
It uses a single helical screw, which is contained

122
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:45,800
within a close-clearance cylindrical housing.

123
00:07:45,900 --> 00:07:49,200
The lower end of the housing is submerged in the liquid.

124
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:52,200
The whole assembly is inclined at an angle.

125
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:54,280
As you start to rotate the screw,

126
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:59,520
it moves liquid up the screw to the point of discharge.

127
00:07:59,620 --> 00:08:02,020
Farmers used screw pumps to lift water

128
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:06,660
into irrigation ditches, which fed their crops.

129
00:08:06,661 --> 00:08:11,660
Over 2,000 years later, this ingenious pump is spinning its

130
00:08:11,760 --> 00:08:16,600
magic at the Henrico County Sewage Facility in Virginia.

131
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:20,400
The screw pump design prevents clogging, making it an ideal

132
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:23,440
pump for raising wastewater, which can be littered with

133
00:08:23,540 --> 00:08:24,680
debris.

134
00:08:24,780 --> 00:08:28,340
This screw pump behind me is used to transmit sewage from

135
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:33,280
the level to my left up to an elevation 30 feet high.

136
00:08:33,281 --> 00:08:37,680
And that sewage then flows by gravity to another building

137
00:08:37,780 --> 00:08:40,280
where centrifugal pumps, a different type of pump,

138
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:43,020
pumps it nine miles to our wastewater treatment

139
00:08:43,120 --> 00:08:43,820
facility.

140
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:49,420
Screw pumps aren't your typical rotary pump.

141
00:08:49,520 --> 00:08:50,940
The majority of them are primarily

142
00:08:51,040 --> 00:08:54,740
used to move thick liquids at high pressure.

143
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:58,040
But no matter the viscosity, all rotary pumps work

144
00:08:58,140 --> 00:09:01,780
towards the same goal, trap a fixed volume of fluid

145
00:09:01,781 --> 00:09:03,580
and displace it into a receiving pipe.

146
00:09:06,580 --> 00:09:08,780
But screw pumps had their shortcomings.

147
00:09:08,781 --> 00:09:11,420
They had to be positioned at an angle to work,

148
00:09:11,421 --> 00:09:14,120
so they weren't very effective at raising water from wells.

149
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:23,140
The solution was the piston pump, introduced in 200 BC,

150
00:09:23,141 --> 00:09:25,240
around the same time as the Archimedean screw.

151
00:09:27,780 --> 00:09:30,280
Just like a hand-powered water pump,

152
00:09:30,281 --> 00:09:33,920
early piston pumps lifted water from low-lying aquifers

153
00:09:34,020 --> 00:09:37,760
by creating a vacuum inside the pump.

154
00:09:37,860 --> 00:09:40,460
When a worker raised the piston with a hand crank,

155
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:43,660
a valve at the bottom of the pump opened,

156
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:47,660
and atmospheric pressure forced water inside.

157
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:50,600
When the piston was lowered, the valve closed,

158
00:09:50,700 --> 00:09:52,040
and the water passed through a valve

159
00:09:52,140 --> 00:09:54,340
at the top of the piston.

160
00:09:54,440 --> 00:09:57,640
As the piston was raised again, it forced water out of the pump.

161
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:03,180
The drawback to the ancient and modern piston pump

162
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:06,920
is that it could only lift water 32 feet.

163
00:10:07,020 --> 00:10:09,520
Above that height, the pressure inside the pump

164
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:12,960
is greater than atmospheric pressure.

165
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:18,260
In 1580 AD, Germans, looking to drain groundwater out of mines,

166
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:20,740
devised a clever scheme to bypass this problem.

167
00:10:24,600 --> 00:10:28,280
They used a dual-level piston pump system.

168
00:10:28,281 --> 00:10:30,920
Two pumps lifted the water to a trough,

169
00:10:31,020 --> 00:10:33,160
and then two additional pistons brought the water

170
00:10:33,260 --> 00:10:34,720
from the trough to the surface.

171
00:10:40,020 --> 00:10:44,360
Piston pumps and screw pumps were effective in raising water.

172
00:10:44,460 --> 00:10:46,400
But absent the help of gravity, they

173
00:10:46,500 --> 00:10:49,200
couldn't pump water efficiently across the land.

174
00:10:49,300 --> 00:10:51,700
In the 17th century, the centrifugal pump

175
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:52,980
gave promise of a solution.

176
00:10:56,100 --> 00:10:58,280
By adding velocity to the water with its surface,

177
00:10:58,281 --> 00:11:03,280
impeller, centrifugals could pump water against gravity.

178
00:11:03,380 --> 00:11:05,280
But lacking a powerful and steady driver

179
00:11:05,380 --> 00:11:08,120
to spin its impeller, the centrifugal pump could never

180
00:11:08,220 --> 00:11:09,020
achieve its potential.

181
00:11:11,820 --> 00:11:15,300
That changed with the emergence of diesel and electric motors

182
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:18,340
in the 20th century.

183
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:22,040
The centrifugal pump came into its own.

184
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:24,280
Engineers could now use centrifugal pumps

185
00:11:24,380 --> 00:11:28,280
to transport water over great heights and vast distances.

186
00:11:28,281 --> 00:11:30,520
such as those that brought water to Southern California.

187
00:11:36,180 --> 00:11:38,080
Centrifugal also became a vital tool

188
00:11:38,081 --> 00:11:42,520
for pumping water and slurries in mining operations,

189
00:11:42,521 --> 00:11:43,980
a practice that continues today.

190
00:11:47,340 --> 00:11:50,160
In the state of Florida, the Mosaic Mining Company

191
00:11:50,161 --> 00:11:52,960
uses centrifugal pumps to process one of the world's

192
00:11:52,961 --> 00:11:57,520
most essential minerals, phosphate.

193
00:11:57,521 --> 00:11:59,560
Florida is one of the leading producers of phosphate

194
00:11:59,660 --> 00:12:00,660
in the world.

195
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:03,520
And without phosphate, which is a primary ingredient

196
00:12:03,620 --> 00:12:06,360
for fertilizer, we couldn't feed the world.

197
00:12:09,760 --> 00:12:11,560
Before the phosphate can be pumped,

198
00:12:11,660 --> 00:12:13,200
it has to be prepped.

199
00:12:13,300 --> 00:12:15,600
And that requires a carefully orchestrated dance

200
00:12:15,700 --> 00:12:17,880
between a mammoth dragline shovel

201
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:19,480
and a high-pressure pit gun.

202
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:30,420
We start off with a drag line.

203
00:12:30,520 --> 00:12:32,820
He pre-strips the overburden.

204
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:35,320
After he's pre-stripped the overburden,

205
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:38,260
we're down to the matrix.

206
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:40,960
The matrix of clay, sand, and phosphate

207
00:12:41,060 --> 00:12:45,580
is fed to the well and pounded into slurry.

208
00:12:45,680 --> 00:12:47,780
The water for this liquid battering ram

209
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:51,780
is pumped from a reservoir four miles away.

210
00:12:51,880 --> 00:12:54,140
This is a 22-inch,

211
00:12:54,141 --> 00:12:59,140
This is a 22-inch hydraulic stage pump, high-pressure pump.

212
00:12:59,240 --> 00:13:02,140
We're pumping about 200 pounds of pressure at this pump.

213
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:04,280
You can actually feel the ground shaking here

214
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:05,780
from the velocity of the water.

215
00:13:08,920 --> 00:13:10,960
Two booster pumps adjacent to the gun

216
00:13:11,060 --> 00:13:13,460
push the pressure up even further

217
00:13:13,560 --> 00:13:18,000
to a pulverizing 240 pounds per square inch.

218
00:13:18,100 --> 00:13:20,060
If you were to stand in front of it,

219
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:23,040
it would probably cut you in half.

220
00:13:23,041 --> 00:13:27,040
Once the pit gun has turned the matrix into slurry,

221
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:29,840
it's ready to be pumped to its transfer point,

222
00:13:29,940 --> 00:13:33,280
nine miles away.

223
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:36,880
A centrifugal pump that boasts a 62-inch impeller

224
00:13:36,980 --> 00:13:38,980
will handle the first leg of that job.

225
00:13:42,100 --> 00:13:48,100
The impeller turns at 350 RPMs, pumping about 18,000 gallons

226
00:13:48,200 --> 00:13:49,840
a minute.

227
00:13:49,841 --> 00:13:54,880
We're pumping about 40% solids, and the rest is water.

228
00:13:54,980 --> 00:13:56,980
That's the only way we can move the phosphate

229
00:13:57,080 --> 00:13:58,080
to the processing plant.

230
00:14:01,380 --> 00:14:06,080
The first pump will drive the slurry four miles.

231
00:14:06,180 --> 00:14:08,700
Nine auxiliary pumps keep the mix moving.

232
00:14:12,200 --> 00:14:16,440
The final centrifugal pump lifts the slurry 170 feet up

233
00:14:16,540 --> 00:14:19,840
to the top of the processing plant.

234
00:14:19,841 --> 00:14:24,840
Now gravity takes over, sending the slurry cascading through

235
00:14:24,940 --> 00:14:28,080
a series of grates that gradually isolate the phosphate

236
00:14:28,180 --> 00:14:29,220
from the matrix.

237
00:14:31,720 --> 00:14:34,520
The finished product is then transported along a conveyor

238
00:14:34,620 --> 00:14:37,520
belt and dumped onto the product pile.

239
00:14:40,660 --> 00:14:44,900
Each year in the United States, centrifugal pumps transport 34

240
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,840
million tons of phosphate matrix to processing plants like this

241
00:14:48,841 --> 00:14:50,640
like this one.

242
00:14:50,740 --> 00:14:53,080
But these pumps aren't just for transporting water

243
00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:54,080
and water slurries.

244
00:14:57,580 --> 00:15:00,080
The world depends on centrifugal pumps

245
00:15:00,180 --> 00:15:02,780
to quench the thirst of an unending cavalcade

246
00:15:02,880 --> 00:15:04,280
of motorized giants.

247
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,300
Welcome to Los Angeles International Airport.

248
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:20,840
The fifth busiest airport in the world.

249
00:15:23,980 --> 00:15:27,980
In a given year, approximately 650,000 airplanes land

250
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:28,980
and take off from here.

251
00:15:32,580 --> 00:15:37,100
Each time a plane lands at LAX, it not only takes on passengers,

252
00:15:37,200 --> 00:15:43,900
it takes on fuel, which is pumped from a single enormous

253
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,840
gas station.

254
00:15:46,841 --> 00:15:55,800
The goal of LaxFuel essentially is to provide clean, dry fuel on time to the airlines at Los Angeles International Airport.

255
00:15:56,600 --> 00:16:03,380
We supply Jet A petroleum to about 99.9% of the airline customers.

256
00:16:03,860 --> 00:16:13,920
On a given day, we receive approximately 100,000 to 110,000 barrels a day, and we dispense about 100 to 110,000 a day.

257
00:16:13,921 --> 00:16:18,320
LAX fuel, which is located on the rim of the airport,

258
00:16:18,420 --> 00:16:20,720
receives its stock via centrifugal pump

259
00:16:20,820 --> 00:16:22,660
from four separate refiners.

260
00:16:25,660 --> 00:16:28,860
The jet fuel is held in one of 15 storage tanks,

261
00:16:28,960 --> 00:16:33,040
located in what LAX fuel calls its tank farm.

262
00:16:33,140 --> 00:16:35,100
From there, another set of centrifugal pumps

263
00:16:35,200 --> 00:16:37,440
will carry the fuel to the various terminal gates

264
00:16:37,540 --> 00:16:38,340
at the airport.

265
00:16:40,240 --> 00:16:43,920
The entire operation is computer controlled.

266
00:16:43,921 --> 00:16:50,720
Our computer control room is commonly referred to as the SCADA system, or surveillance control

267
00:16:50,721 --> 00:16:52,854
and data acquisition system.

268
00:16:52,855 --> 00:16:57,960
On the SCADA system, we're essentially monitoring our flow rates of

269
00:16:57,961 --> 00:17:04,780
product coming into the facility. Because it's a high-profile target for terrorism,

270
00:17:05,780 --> 00:17:07,752
security is paramount here.

271
00:17:07,753 --> 00:17:12,280
Black's fuel surveillance system monitors every square inch of

272
00:17:12,281 --> 00:17:14,980
the grounds, seeking out any unwanted visitors.

273
00:17:19,380 --> 00:17:21,620
Once the fuel is safely in the tank,

274
00:17:21,621 --> 00:17:23,820
it has to be pumped into the planes at the airfield.

275
00:17:27,900 --> 00:17:32,100
Fuel exits the storage tank by gravity at 20 psi

276
00:17:32,101 --> 00:17:35,260
through a 24-inch diameter discharge pipe.

277
00:17:35,261 --> 00:17:38,240
It then flows to a centrifugal pump.

278
00:17:38,241 --> 00:17:42,740
pressurizes the jet fuel to 150 psi.

279
00:17:42,840 --> 00:17:46,240
That's 10 times greater than atmospheric pressure.

280
00:17:46,340 --> 00:17:48,920
It will maintain this pressure ceiling for the fuel's entire

281
00:17:49,020 --> 00:17:50,980
journey to the airfield.

282
00:17:51,080 --> 00:17:55,820
There's 18 pumps that dispense 1,200 gallons per minute.

283
00:17:55,920 --> 00:18:03,060
They're 150 horsepower pumps with an RPM rating of 3,600.

284
00:18:03,160 --> 00:18:05,060
From the pump, the pressurized jet fuel

285
00:18:05,061 --> 00:18:08,760
through a 20-inch pipeline.

286
00:18:08,860 --> 00:18:11,860
The pipe splits off into a series of smaller conduits

287
00:18:11,960 --> 00:18:16,000
that lead to the various airport terminal gates.

288
00:18:16,100 --> 00:18:18,300
Underneath the tarmac at each gate

289
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:22,740
is a capped outlet fitted with a valve called a hydrant.

290
00:18:22,840 --> 00:18:25,120
The fuel will remain here at pressure

291
00:18:25,220 --> 00:18:27,120
until a plane is ready for fueling.

292
00:18:31,520 --> 00:18:34,020
Centrifugal pumps are a good pump.

293
00:18:34,021 --> 00:18:37,520
because they are able to supply the airport

294
00:18:37,620 --> 00:18:41,920
with a constant delivery pressure.

295
00:18:42,020 --> 00:18:44,560
When an airplane is ready to take on fuel,

296
00:18:44,660 --> 00:18:47,260
a truck equipped with a 50-foot-long hose

297
00:18:47,360 --> 00:18:50,500
arrives at the gate.

298
00:18:50,600 --> 00:18:53,380
A technician connects the hose to the hydrant,

299
00:18:53,480 --> 00:18:58,440
opening its valve and releasing the pressurized jet fuel.

300
00:18:58,540 --> 00:19:00,840
You just go ahead and pull the lever back,

301
00:19:00,940 --> 00:19:02,320
and it locks it in place.

302
00:19:02,321 --> 00:19:06,960
Now you're set so that the fuel can flow right through the nozzle.

303
00:19:07,060 --> 00:19:10,120
We just go up on the lift, and we use the nozzles that we have

304
00:19:10,220 --> 00:19:13,700
up there to hook it up to the fuel panel.

305
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:21,140
I put in 6,376 gallons, and it took about maybe 20 minutes.

306
00:19:21,240 --> 00:19:22,880
That's a tank full that would certainly max out

307
00:19:22,980 --> 00:19:23,680
your credit card.

308
00:19:26,440 --> 00:19:29,420
Fueling planes is a constant at LAX.

309
00:19:29,520 --> 00:19:32,320
So is making sure that the fuel never runs out.

310
00:19:32,321 --> 00:19:36,120
We are essentially like a gas station at the airport,

311
00:19:36,220 --> 00:19:40,460
whereas we receive the fuel, we monitor the product quality

312
00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:45,060
of the fuel, and we dispense the fuel to the airplanes.

313
00:19:47,260 --> 00:19:49,980
LaxFuel may be one of the biggest gas stations

314
00:19:50,080 --> 00:19:55,680
in the world, but there are thousands of smaller street

315
00:19:55,780 --> 00:20:00,220
corner stations that also rely on centrifugal pumps.

316
00:20:00,221 --> 00:20:03,220
The Dresser Wayne Company, based in Austin, Texas,

317
00:20:03,221 --> 00:20:06,220
has been designing and building equipment for fueling stations

318
00:20:06,221 --> 00:20:09,220
for 115 years.

319
00:20:09,221 --> 00:20:13,220
If you were to leave right now to go out to a gas station,

320
00:20:13,221 --> 00:20:17,220
there's a 50% chance that you would be getting a Dresser Wayne

321
00:20:17,221 --> 00:20:20,220
dispenser that was made right here in Austin, Texas.

322
00:20:20,221 --> 00:20:23,220
We start from the ground up, basically filling the structure

323
00:20:23,221 --> 00:20:26,220
frame with side columns, and you draw the sheet mail around it,

324
00:20:26,221 --> 00:20:29,220
and all the electronic components.

325
00:20:29,221 --> 00:20:30,720
components.

326
00:20:30,820 --> 00:20:33,220
In this maze of pipes and wires,

327
00:20:33,320 --> 00:20:35,520
you'd be hard pressed to find a pump.

328
00:20:35,620 --> 00:20:37,220
And for good reason.

329
00:20:37,320 --> 00:20:39,960
The so-called gas pump that you hook up to your car

330
00:20:40,060 --> 00:20:41,860
isn't really a pump at all.

331
00:20:41,960 --> 00:20:46,680
There's a misconception that the gas pump that you use

332
00:20:46,780 --> 00:20:51,180
is actually pumping the gas into your car.

333
00:20:51,280 --> 00:20:54,280
Actually, the pump, which is known as a submersible,

334
00:20:54,380 --> 00:20:59,220
is located in an underground storage tank at the gas station.

335
00:20:59,221 --> 00:21:01,860
In a submersible pump, the pump and its motor

336
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:06,260
are joined together in a hermetically sealed housing.

337
00:21:06,360 --> 00:21:10,260
This allows the unit to be immersed in liquid.

338
00:21:10,360 --> 00:21:12,560
When the customer selects a grade of gasoline,

339
00:21:12,660 --> 00:21:16,000
the pump's motor turns on, rotating a rod which

340
00:21:16,100 --> 00:21:18,340
spins an impeller.

341
00:21:18,440 --> 00:21:21,140
The spinning of the impeller draws gasoline from the underground

342
00:21:21,240 --> 00:21:25,320
tank and through a pipeline to the dispenser.

343
00:21:25,420 --> 00:21:28,040
The nozzle, which most people believe to be the pump,

344
00:21:28,041 --> 00:21:30,980
to be the pump is actually a valve.

345
00:21:30,981 --> 00:21:33,140
When it's opened, the pressurized gasoline

346
00:21:33,141 --> 00:21:37,240
flows freely into the gas tank.

347
00:21:37,241 --> 00:21:39,980
The main role of the dispenser, that familiar monolith

348
00:21:39,981 --> 00:21:43,560
that houses the nozzle, is to calculate the volume of gasoline

349
00:21:43,561 --> 00:21:46,060
passing through it.

350
00:21:46,061 --> 00:21:48,520
Once the fueling comes from the dismergeable pump,

351
00:21:48,521 --> 00:21:49,860
it's going to come up to the I-meter,

352
00:21:49,861 --> 00:21:52,160
and it's going to account for every drop of the fuel that

353
00:21:52,161 --> 00:21:55,160
comes through the dispenser.

354
00:21:55,161 --> 00:21:57,000
The fuel dispenser meter consists

355
00:21:57,001 --> 00:21:59,900
pistons attached to a crankshaft.

356
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:03,500
With each cycle, the pistons move a precise amount of gasoline

357
00:22:03,600 --> 00:22:06,000
through the dispenser.

358
00:22:06,100 --> 00:22:10,040
The rotating crankshaft triggers sensors that transmit pulses

359
00:22:10,140 --> 00:22:13,040
to a central processing unit.

360
00:22:13,140 --> 00:22:16,880
Each pulse represents a measured amount of gasoline, which the

361
00:22:16,980 --> 00:22:20,760
CPU translates into a digital number that's displayed on the

362
00:22:20,860 --> 00:22:21,860
dispenser.

363
00:22:24,360 --> 00:22:27,000
Metering pumps ensure that you get what you pay for.

364
00:22:27,001 --> 00:22:28,240
at the pump.

365
00:22:28,340 --> 00:22:31,640
That wasn't always the case.

366
00:22:31,740 --> 00:22:35,000
In the early 1900s, figuring out how much gas

367
00:22:35,100 --> 00:22:38,540
you were pumping was more of a guessing game.

368
00:22:38,640 --> 00:22:43,120
This dispenser here is what we call our 300 series,

369
00:22:43,220 --> 00:22:46,960
which was developed around the World War I era.

370
00:22:47,060 --> 00:22:49,160
You would actually make a selection on the dial,

371
00:22:49,260 --> 00:22:52,460
and in so doing, you would make a predetermined selection

372
00:22:52,560 --> 00:22:54,060
anywhere from one to five gallons.

373
00:22:54,061 --> 00:22:57,600
There was a small positive displacement or piston pump

374
00:22:57,601 --> 00:22:59,060
contained within the cabinet.

375
00:22:59,061 --> 00:23:01,600
The attendant would turn a hand crank up

376
00:23:01,601 --> 00:23:03,600
to the predetermined amount.

377
00:23:03,601 --> 00:23:06,480
Then he would actually release the hand crank

378
00:23:06,481 --> 00:23:09,100
and cause the pump to carry the liquid up

379
00:23:09,101 --> 00:23:11,940
through the supply pipe into the visible bowl.

380
00:23:11,941 --> 00:23:14,020
If you wanted to release the contents of the globe,

381
00:23:14,021 --> 00:23:15,720
there was a handle over here,

382
00:23:15,721 --> 00:23:19,020
and you would put this in the car, and off you would go.

383
00:23:19,021 --> 00:23:21,860
Not as precise as what we are used to today,

384
00:23:21,861 --> 00:23:25,860
but for the time being, it served very well.

385
00:23:25,960 --> 00:23:27,860
The first calculating fuel dispenser

386
00:23:27,960 --> 00:23:29,860
appeared in 1933.

387
00:23:34,400 --> 00:23:36,540
Today, state-of-the-art fueling stations

388
00:23:36,640 --> 00:23:38,480
come equipped with plasma screens,

389
00:23:38,580 --> 00:23:41,140
real-time weather and news reports,

390
00:23:41,240 --> 00:23:43,620
and commercials geared to entice the customer

391
00:23:43,720 --> 00:23:47,360
into the station's minimart.

392
00:23:47,460 --> 00:23:48,360
Pizza anyone?

393
00:23:51,860 --> 00:23:55,100
Further bells and whistles will undoubtedly follow,

394
00:23:55,200 --> 00:23:57,000
but one thing will never change.

395
00:23:57,100 --> 00:24:00,600
The act of pumping gas will always demand a pump.

396
00:24:04,100 --> 00:24:07,100
Diesel and electric motors provided a much-needed boost

397
00:24:07,200 --> 00:24:10,420
to the centrifugal pump industry.

398
00:24:10,520 --> 00:24:14,180
But what they did for piston pumps transformed the simple

399
00:24:14,280 --> 00:24:17,560
squirt bottle into a high-pressure pump

400
00:24:17,660 --> 00:24:19,120
that can cut through steel.

401
00:24:21,860 --> 00:24:28,860
Push the plunger, wash your hands, or pump up your tires.

402
00:24:28,861 --> 00:24:31,860
These household pumps all use pistons that

403
00:24:31,861 --> 00:24:33,860
displace liquid or air.

404
00:24:33,861 --> 00:24:36,860
Simple.

405
00:24:36,861 --> 00:24:39,860
Now, add a high-energy power source,

406
00:24:39,861 --> 00:24:43,860
and you've got a piston pump on steroids.

407
00:24:43,861 --> 00:24:48,860
This is a 40,000-PSI-3-inch pump.

408
00:24:48,861 --> 00:24:53,520
This is a 40,000-PSI dump-style hand lance.

409
00:24:53,760 --> 00:24:59,040
It's used everywhere from general cleaning to precision cutting.

410
00:25:00,540 --> 00:25:03,860
And we are going to do some blasting today with this lance.

411
00:25:07,680 --> 00:25:11,220
You might not want to stand in front of the business end of this pump.

412
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:18,160
While centrifugal pumps move large volumes of fluid at lower pressure,

413
00:25:18,860 --> 00:25:22,060
Piston pumps help generate pressures that will cut through

414
00:25:22,160 --> 00:25:24,600
a 4x4 like a hot night through butter.

415
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:33,180
These high-speed pistons are pumping 60 gallons of water

416
00:25:33,280 --> 00:25:34,080
per minute.

417
00:25:36,140 --> 00:25:38,440
When the water passes through a constricted orifice

418
00:25:38,540 --> 00:25:43,520
like a nozzle, its pressure and velocity is amped up.

419
00:25:43,620 --> 00:25:48,860
Water exits at 1,500 miles per hour.

420
00:25:48,861 --> 00:25:50,860
The fuel is a little bit more.

421
00:25:54,860 --> 00:25:58,860
Hooking the pump up to a 600-horsepower diesel engine

422
00:25:58,861 --> 00:26:01,860
that drives the pistons and adding a mild abrasive to the

423
00:26:01,861 --> 00:26:04,860
water allows it to cut through steel.

424
00:26:08,860 --> 00:26:12,860
The velocity of the water driving the abrasive through the

425
00:26:12,861 --> 00:26:14,860
metal is what does the cutting.

426
00:26:14,861 --> 00:26:16,860
The rest of it is the high-pressure water

427
00:26:16,861 --> 00:26:20,060
and that media injected into the stream.

428
00:26:20,061 --> 00:26:22,660
Using this method, you can cut through anywhere

429
00:26:22,661 --> 00:26:26,060
from a quarter inch to several inches, four, five, six inches

430
00:26:26,061 --> 00:26:26,761
thick of metal.

431
00:26:33,600 --> 00:26:37,900
Although pistons generate velocity and pressure,

432
00:26:37,901 --> 00:26:42,620
it's the nozzle that dramatically increases both.

433
00:26:42,621 --> 00:26:45,240
During the California gold rush, miners

434
00:26:45,241 --> 00:26:48,740
concept to devastating effect.

435
00:26:48,840 --> 00:26:52,340
They connected hoses to streams at high elevations,

436
00:26:52,440 --> 00:26:55,680
letting gravity instead of pumps create tremendous pressure.

437
00:26:58,780 --> 00:27:00,380
Nozzles fitted to the ends of the hoses

438
00:27:00,480 --> 00:27:04,000
blasted the water against the hillsides, demolishing them

439
00:27:04,100 --> 00:27:07,300
and releasing gold.

440
00:27:07,400 --> 00:27:09,000
Hydraulic mining, as it was called,

441
00:27:09,100 --> 00:27:12,800
had a disastrous impact on the environment.

442
00:27:12,900 --> 00:27:14,700
Although it was outlawed in California,

443
00:27:14,701 --> 00:27:18,700
in 1855, the practice of hydraulic mining continued

444
00:27:18,800 --> 00:27:21,200
in other states for another century.

445
00:27:24,100 --> 00:27:27,240
The pushy nature of motorized piston pumps isn't reserved

446
00:27:27,340 --> 00:27:30,540
for high-pressure blasting.

447
00:27:30,640 --> 00:27:32,780
A pump jack uses the same principle

448
00:27:32,880 --> 00:27:37,260
to bring oil from underneath the ground to the surface.

449
00:27:37,360 --> 00:27:41,060
The pump is typically powered by an electric motor.

450
00:27:41,160 --> 00:27:44,700
The power driver turns a pair of cranks, which raise an

451
00:27:44,701 --> 00:27:46,200
the pump beam.

452
00:27:46,300 --> 00:27:48,840
The beam converts the rotary motion of the cranks

453
00:27:48,940 --> 00:27:51,200
into a vertical reciprocating motion

454
00:27:51,300 --> 00:27:53,700
that drives the piston pump.

455
00:27:53,800 --> 00:27:57,820
With each cycle, a pump jack can lift 5 to 40 liters of fluid,

456
00:27:57,920 --> 00:28:01,480
depending on the diameter and length of the piston.

457
00:28:01,580 --> 00:28:04,320
Pump jacks date back to the 1920s.

458
00:28:04,420 --> 00:28:08,020
Today, tens of thousands of them work the countryside,

459
00:28:08,120 --> 00:28:10,560
pumping oil relentlessly.

460
00:28:10,660 --> 00:28:12,320
Their rhythmic and repetitive motion

461
00:28:12,321 --> 00:28:17,660
has led to the nicknames thirsty bird and nodding donkey.

462
00:28:17,760 --> 00:28:20,020
While a pump jack can move oil, you'll

463
00:28:20,120 --> 00:28:23,140
need another kind of pump to move really viscous liquids,

464
00:28:23,240 --> 00:28:23,940
like peanut butter.

465
00:28:27,840 --> 00:28:29,000
That's a rotary pump.

466
00:28:32,780 --> 00:28:35,740
Rotary pumps like to work behind the scenes,

467
00:28:35,840 --> 00:28:38,140
pumping oil to lubricate your car engine

468
00:28:38,240 --> 00:28:41,020
and pressurizing fluid that extends cylinders

469
00:28:41,021 --> 00:28:43,020
for hydraulic, earth-moving monsters.

470
00:28:47,020 --> 00:28:49,520
Viking Pump in Cedar Falls, Iowa,

471
00:28:49,620 --> 00:28:51,760
is one of the oldest rotary pump manufacturers

472
00:28:51,860 --> 00:28:54,760
in the United States.

473
00:28:54,860 --> 00:28:58,280
They opened their doors in 1911, selling a new invention

474
00:28:58,380 --> 00:29:01,080
called the internal gear pump.

475
00:29:01,180 --> 00:29:02,980
The gentleman that invented the internal gear pump,

476
00:29:03,080 --> 00:29:05,420
principal, his name was Jens Nielsen.

477
00:29:05,520 --> 00:29:07,780
He had a rock quarry here in Cedar Falls.

478
00:29:07,880 --> 00:29:10,080
He couldn't get the rocks out because it was full of water.

479
00:29:10,081 --> 00:29:12,820
So he came up with the invention of an internal gear pump

480
00:29:12,920 --> 00:29:15,020
to actually lift water out of his rock quarry

481
00:29:15,120 --> 00:29:17,860
and evacuate the water.

482
00:29:17,960 --> 00:29:19,720
Today's internal gear pumps still

483
00:29:19,820 --> 00:29:21,020
pump thin liquids.

484
00:29:21,120 --> 00:29:23,300
But their ability to pump thick, gooey material,

485
00:29:23,400 --> 00:29:29,000
like chocolate, distinguishes them from other pumps.

486
00:29:29,100 --> 00:29:30,460
So how do they do it?

487
00:29:30,560 --> 00:29:32,340
This is an internal gear pump.

488
00:29:32,440 --> 00:29:34,840
It consists of an outer gear called a rotor.

489
00:29:34,940 --> 00:29:37,300
You can see the teeth along here.

490
00:29:37,301 --> 00:29:42,753
an idler. It's this portion right here. The rotor drives the idler.

491
00:29:42,754 --> 00:29:44,300
As the rotor turns,

492
00:29:46,060 --> 00:29:48,224
you can see the gears are coming out of mesh.

493
00:29:48,225 --> 00:29:50,340
The liquid is drawn in, comes in between the

494
00:29:50,341 --> 00:29:53,564
teeth, fills the cavities as they come out of mesh.

495
00:29:53,565 --> 00:29:55,840
This part here is called a crescent.

496
00:29:55,841 --> 00:29:56,990
It seals the liquid in.

497
00:29:56,991 --> 00:30:00,240
As the rotor continues to turn, it transfers the liquid around to

498
00:30:00,241 --> 00:30:01,534
this side of the pump.

499
00:30:01,535 --> 00:30:05,300
As the teeth come into mesh, it pushes liquid out the port here,

500
00:30:05,301 --> 00:30:09,540
the discharge port with a continuous flow.

501
00:30:09,640 --> 00:30:11,840
Viking Pump builds internal gear pumps

502
00:30:11,940 --> 00:30:15,140
from the ground up.

503
00:30:15,240 --> 00:30:17,540
The process starts in their foundry,

504
00:30:17,640 --> 00:30:20,140
where superheated metal is poured into a sand mold.

505
00:30:23,360 --> 00:30:32,060
It's then separated from the mold, cooled, cleaned,

506
00:30:32,061 --> 00:30:35,600
and sent next door to be machined and assembled.

507
00:30:38,560 --> 00:30:41,300
This internal gear pump will be used to pump asphalt.

508
00:30:44,180 --> 00:30:46,260
With nearly 100 years under their belt,

509
00:30:46,740 --> 00:30:50,240
Viking pumps sees no shortage in the worldwide demand for rotary pumps.

510
00:30:50,560 --> 00:30:53,160
I think there's always going to be pumps as long as there's liquids,

511
00:30:53,620 --> 00:30:55,440
liquids to transfer one place to another.

512
00:30:55,560 --> 00:30:57,140
I just don't think it's ever going to run out.

513
00:30:57,880 --> 00:31:00,000
No matter the viscosity of the liquid,

514
00:31:00,001 --> 00:31:04,840
there's a pump that can suck, push, or lift it to its

515
00:31:04,940 --> 00:31:05,640
destination.

516
00:31:08,940 --> 00:31:11,980
But there's one liquid we drink nearly every day that

517
00:31:12,080 --> 00:31:17,860
demands a full arsenal of pumps to get it from the farm

518
00:31:17,960 --> 00:31:18,760
to the fridge.

519
00:31:23,460 --> 00:31:30,000
It's wholesome, nutritious, and a staple of any healthy diet.

520
00:31:30,001 --> 00:31:32,000
Got milk?

521
00:31:32,100 --> 00:31:34,640
Americans sure do.

522
00:31:34,740 --> 00:31:39,240
They consume over 20 billion gallons of it each year.

523
00:31:39,340 --> 00:31:45,240
But getting it from here to here

524
00:31:45,340 --> 00:31:50,060
is a multi-step process that requires an assembly of pumps.

525
00:31:50,160 --> 00:31:53,160
Well, pumps start off playing an important part at the farm.

526
00:31:53,260 --> 00:31:57,900
Pumps are necessary at the farm to bring the milk from the cow

527
00:31:57,901 --> 00:32:01,500
to the holding tank at the farm.

528
00:32:01,600 --> 00:32:03,700
The majority of dairies in the United States

529
00:32:03,800 --> 00:32:06,700
employ milkers that use hand-operated machines

530
00:32:06,800 --> 00:32:08,640
to extract milk from the cow.

531
00:32:11,240 --> 00:32:15,060
But at Mason-Dixon Farms in Gettysburg, Pennsylvania,

532
00:32:15,160 --> 00:32:18,760
they've got 4,000 cows.

533
00:32:18,860 --> 00:32:22,020
Their new milker is a fully automatic pumping bot

534
00:32:22,120 --> 00:32:25,200
that never gets tired and won't get a sore back.

535
00:32:27,900 --> 00:32:30,140
When the cow enters the milking stall,

536
00:32:30,240 --> 00:32:32,540
the machine's hydraulic arm covers the teat

537
00:32:32,640 --> 00:32:35,540
with a suction cup.

538
00:32:35,640 --> 00:32:38,540
A rotary pump is preferred because it easily generates

539
00:32:38,640 --> 00:32:40,640
a gentle vacuum.

540
00:32:40,740 --> 00:32:43,540
This keeps the cup in place and also replicates

541
00:32:43,640 --> 00:32:45,160
the pressure of a sucking cow.

542
00:32:47,560 --> 00:32:50,560
The milk is typically pumped directly into a tanker truck

543
00:32:50,660 --> 00:32:52,560
that hauls it to a processing plant.

544
00:32:54,760 --> 00:32:57,900
The Altadena certified dairy in La Praia

545
00:32:57,901 --> 00:33:01,900
is one of the busiest processing plants in the state.

546
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:04,900
We receive approximately 50 tanker loads a day.

547
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:06,900
Each one holds roughly 6,000 gallons.

548
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:09,240
Those come in here directly off the farm.

549
00:33:09,340 --> 00:33:13,540
We process 300,000 gallons per day.

550
00:33:13,640 --> 00:33:17,160
Altadena has 300 to 400 pumps here to do the job.

551
00:33:19,860 --> 00:33:22,760
But before the first drop of milk is pumped out of the trucks,

552
00:33:22,860 --> 00:33:24,460
it has to be tested.

553
00:33:24,560 --> 00:33:26,260
When the truck first gets here,

554
00:33:26,261 --> 00:33:29,300
the receiver will go up on top of the tanker, open the dome,

555
00:33:29,400 --> 00:33:31,560
pull a sample that we used to send to the lab

556
00:33:31,660 --> 00:33:33,100
for quality checking.

557
00:33:33,200 --> 00:33:34,500
He'll come down the steps.

558
00:33:34,600 --> 00:33:36,300
We'll send the sample through a pneumatic tube

559
00:33:36,400 --> 00:33:38,600
that goes to the lab.

560
00:33:38,700 --> 00:33:40,880
After the milk receives a clean bill of health,

561
00:33:40,980 --> 00:33:43,880
it's time for the centrifugal pump to take center stage.

562
00:33:46,880 --> 00:33:49,380
Ideal for pumping large volumes of liquid,

563
00:33:49,480 --> 00:33:53,060
a centrifugal pump will quickly suck the trucks dry.

564
00:33:53,160 --> 00:33:55,780
We're pumping about a 12,000 gallon

565
00:33:55,781 --> 00:33:57,020
an hour to this pump.

566
00:33:57,120 --> 00:33:58,580
It's important we have the top vent open.

567
00:33:58,680 --> 00:34:01,920
The pumps are packing fast enough to where it will literally

568
00:34:02,020 --> 00:34:04,420
collapse the tank.

569
00:34:04,520 --> 00:34:06,120
Without a pressure release point,

570
00:34:06,220 --> 00:34:08,520
the pump would generate a vacuum that would crush the tanker

571
00:34:08,620 --> 00:34:09,520
like a tin can.

572
00:34:14,640 --> 00:34:17,440
Raw milk travels from the tanker into various storage

573
00:34:17,540 --> 00:34:19,840
silos at the plant.

574
00:34:19,940 --> 00:34:22,580
From there, centrifugal pumps transfer the milk to a holding

575
00:34:22,581 --> 00:34:25,320
to a holding tank and onto a machine that

576
00:34:25,321 --> 00:34:28,880
uses the same centrifugal force principle to separate it

577
00:34:28,881 --> 00:34:31,580
into skim and cream.

578
00:34:31,581 --> 00:34:34,520
It's spinning at about 8,000 RPM driven by motor

579
00:34:34,521 --> 00:34:37,420
going about 4,700 RPM.

580
00:34:37,421 --> 00:34:38,900
The skim milk and the cream are then

581
00:34:38,901 --> 00:34:41,940
whisked away once again by centrifugal pumps

582
00:34:41,941 --> 00:34:43,040
to individual silos.

583
00:34:46,540 --> 00:34:50,580
They will eventually end up here at the batch and blend room,

584
00:34:50,581 --> 00:34:52,820
where they'll be used as ingredients for ice cream

585
00:34:52,920 --> 00:34:54,420
and a variety of milk drinks.

586
00:34:58,820 --> 00:35:01,160
But it takes more than just skim and cream

587
00:35:01,260 --> 00:35:02,860
to make products for your sweet tooth.

588
00:35:06,100 --> 00:35:08,260
Other ingredients headed for the batch and blend room

589
00:35:08,360 --> 00:35:10,700
demand a different breed of pump.

590
00:35:10,800 --> 00:35:13,340
This tank holds high fructose corn sweetener.

591
00:35:13,440 --> 00:35:16,500
What we use it for is going to be the chocolate milks, eggnog,

592
00:35:16,600 --> 00:35:19,000
sweetened cream, ice cream, and other applications.

593
00:35:20,580 --> 00:35:24,320
If you move this thick viscosity liquid too fast,

594
00:35:24,420 --> 00:35:26,720
it'll burst the transit pipes.

595
00:35:26,820 --> 00:35:29,460
A five-horsepower rotary pump sends it along

596
00:35:29,560 --> 00:35:32,960
at a slow, even pace.

597
00:35:33,060 --> 00:35:37,400
With the ingredients mixed, the milk is on the move again.

598
00:35:37,500 --> 00:35:40,340
Centrifugal is delivered to a heat transfer unit,

599
00:35:40,440 --> 00:35:41,700
where the temperature of the milk

600
00:35:41,800 --> 00:35:45,580
is elevated to 160 degrees, killing off any bacteria

601
00:35:45,680 --> 00:35:48,580
or contaminants.

602
00:35:48,581 --> 00:35:52,660
It then travels to a homogenizer that uses reciprocating piston

603
00:35:52,760 --> 00:35:56,260
pumps to hammer the milk into an even consistency.

604
00:35:56,360 --> 00:35:59,560
There's so much pressure on the fat globules of the milk,

605
00:35:59,660 --> 00:36:03,800
it actually implodes the fat globules.

606
00:36:03,900 --> 00:36:06,060
With the fat globules pulverized,

607
00:36:06,160 --> 00:36:08,060
the homogenized and pasteurized milk

608
00:36:08,160 --> 00:36:10,700
is ready to be packaged.

609
00:36:10,800 --> 00:36:15,680
Centrifugal pumps send the milk to different filler stations.

610
00:36:15,780 --> 00:36:18,100
Per gallon containers, milk is pumped into a bowl

611
00:36:18,101 --> 00:36:23,540
into a bowl and then gravity fed into the receptacle.

612
00:36:23,541 --> 00:36:26,980
Smaller cartons demand the precision of a piston pump.

613
00:36:26,981 --> 00:36:29,220
The milk is pumped into these cartons

614
00:36:29,221 --> 00:36:31,520
through a small piston-type pump.

615
00:36:31,521 --> 00:36:34,940
Each station puts four ounces into the carton.

616
00:36:34,941 --> 00:36:39,340
We run approximately a million cartons a week on this filler,

617
00:36:39,341 --> 00:36:44,060
making sure that the product that we send to you every day

618
00:36:44,061 --> 00:36:45,060
is fresh.

619
00:36:48,100 --> 00:36:50,400
Good job.

620
00:36:50,500 --> 00:36:53,600
Pumps not only keep the dairy industry flowing,

621
00:36:53,700 --> 00:36:57,240
they also keep the human body going.

622
00:36:57,340 --> 00:37:01,240
But what happens if your heart fails?

623
00:37:01,340 --> 00:37:04,280
In an office in New York City, Dr. Robert Jarvik

624
00:37:04,380 --> 00:37:07,820
is using over 2,000 years of pump technology

625
00:37:07,920 --> 00:37:11,460
to create a pump that could one day save millions of lives.

626
00:37:18,100 --> 00:37:21,600
To say that we depend on pumps every day of our lives

627
00:37:21,700 --> 00:37:22,800
is profoundly true.

628
00:37:25,540 --> 00:37:30,480
Each day, the human heart pumps about 2,000 gallons of blood.

629
00:37:30,580 --> 00:37:32,340
That adds up to half a billion gallons

630
00:37:32,440 --> 00:37:34,420
in an average lifespan.

631
00:37:34,520 --> 00:37:37,060
The heart is a positive displacement pump.

632
00:37:37,160 --> 00:37:42,060
Its major function is simply to add pressure and flow

633
00:37:42,160 --> 00:37:43,260
to the bloodstream.

634
00:37:43,360 --> 00:37:45,960
It consists of our right side that pumps the blood to the lungs

635
00:37:45,961 --> 00:37:48,600
and left side that pumps the blood to the body.

636
00:37:48,700 --> 00:37:50,700
But just like an industrial pump,

637
00:37:50,800 --> 00:37:53,000
the human heart can lose pressure,

638
00:37:53,100 --> 00:37:55,900
and its pipes can clog.

639
00:37:56,000 --> 00:37:58,400
Every year, approximately 1 million Americans

640
00:37:58,500 --> 00:38:01,240
die from heart disease.

641
00:38:01,340 --> 00:38:06,140
Heart disease means that the heart can't pump enough

642
00:38:06,240 --> 00:38:09,880
in the right way at the right pressure levels.

643
00:38:09,980 --> 00:38:12,380
And therefore, the body suffers for lack

644
00:38:12,480 --> 00:38:15,960
of the necessary circulatory flow.

645
00:38:15,961 --> 00:38:20,140
In the year 2000, Peter Houghton was told that his battle

646
00:38:20,240 --> 00:38:22,760
with heart disease was about to come to an end.

647
00:38:25,300 --> 00:38:27,740
The doctor said, there's nothing further we can do

648
00:38:27,840 --> 00:38:29,180
to treat you.

649
00:38:29,280 --> 00:38:32,840
The drugs are no longer effective.

650
00:38:32,940 --> 00:38:37,080
So I'm afraid you've got to be prepared to die.

651
00:38:37,180 --> 00:38:40,380
Peter Houghton should be dead, but he was pumped back

652
00:38:40,480 --> 00:38:44,780
to life by this, the Jarvik 2000 artificial heart.

653
00:38:44,781 --> 00:38:47,620
What we're trying to do with the Jarvik 2000

654
00:38:47,720 --> 00:38:52,920
is make the simplest, smallest, most reliable compact pump

655
00:38:53,020 --> 00:38:57,020
that'll help the heart do what it needs.

656
00:38:57,120 --> 00:38:59,820
The Jarvik 2000 doesn't replace the heart.

657
00:38:59,920 --> 00:39:02,900
It simply assists with its pumping function.

658
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:05,500
The Jarvik 2000 is a rotary pump

659
00:39:05,600 --> 00:39:08,440
that fits inside the heart's left ventricle.

660
00:39:08,540 --> 00:39:11,400
Underneath its hermetically sealed titanium shell

661
00:39:11,401 --> 00:39:15,000
is a tiny impeller that's turned by a rotor.

662
00:39:15,100 --> 00:39:18,100
As the impeller spins, it pulls blood from the heart

663
00:39:18,200 --> 00:39:21,140
and through a tube that leads to the aorta.

664
00:39:21,240 --> 00:39:25,140
From there, the blood is distributed throughout the body.

665
00:39:25,240 --> 00:39:29,660
The Jarvik 2000 is powered by an external battery pack.

666
00:39:29,760 --> 00:39:32,960
The battery is connected to the pump by a wire that passes

667
00:39:33,060 --> 00:39:37,960
through a connector behind the ear, down the neck,

668
00:39:38,060 --> 00:39:40,500
and through the chest cavity, where

669
00:39:40,501 --> 00:39:44,140
where it's attached to the pump's motor.

670
00:39:44,240 --> 00:39:45,840
This is the battery.

671
00:39:45,940 --> 00:39:48,440
It's got a charge of eight hours.

672
00:39:48,540 --> 00:39:50,940
If I press this and the lights come up,

673
00:39:51,040 --> 00:39:53,840
I can tell roughly how many hours I've got.

674
00:39:53,940 --> 00:39:58,760
Each light is about an hour and a half.

675
00:39:58,860 --> 00:40:00,760
So as you can see from this, I've

676
00:40:00,860 --> 00:40:04,160
got about four and a half hours left.

677
00:40:04,260 --> 00:40:08,000
But that doesn't mean he'll die if the battery runs out.

678
00:40:08,001 --> 00:40:11,200
Houghton's heart, although limited, still beats,

679
00:40:11,300 --> 00:40:13,000
acting as a permanent backup battery.

680
00:40:16,300 --> 00:40:18,440
The predecessor to the Jarvik 2000

681
00:40:18,540 --> 00:40:23,240
was the Jarvik 7, a complete heart transplant device.

682
00:40:23,340 --> 00:40:26,960
In 1982, Barney Clark was the first recipient.

683
00:40:31,660 --> 00:40:34,400
The Jarvik 7, as well as the other artificial hearts that

684
00:40:34,500 --> 00:40:37,560
followed, were rarely used as permanent heart replacement

685
00:40:37,561 --> 00:40:43,800
devices, largely because they were cumbersome and complicated.

686
00:40:43,900 --> 00:40:47,500
The Jarvik 2000 has only one moving part,

687
00:40:47,600 --> 00:40:49,600
its simplicity and diminutive size

688
00:40:49,700 --> 00:40:51,100
separated from its predecessors.

689
00:40:54,320 --> 00:40:56,540
Peter Houghton regulates the speed of his heart pump

690
00:40:56,640 --> 00:40:59,220
through a controller that's connected to the battery pack.

691
00:41:01,960 --> 00:41:05,160
Setting two is, I call it the sleep setting.

692
00:41:05,161 --> 00:41:09,000
Setting three, I call it the ordinary get out and about setting.

693
00:41:09,001 --> 00:41:11,400
Setting four is the hill setting.

694
00:41:11,401 --> 00:41:13,600
Setting five is the mountain setting.

695
00:41:17,480 --> 00:41:20,920
This is a child-sized Jarvik 2000 pump,

696
00:41:20,921 --> 00:41:25,160
just showing the flow of the actual pumping water here, not blood.

697
00:41:25,161 --> 00:41:27,600
But it's very similar for flow.

698
00:41:27,601 --> 00:41:29,840
This is the lower pump speed.

699
00:41:29,841 --> 00:41:32,800
And as I turn the speed up on the controller by hand,

700
00:41:32,801 --> 00:41:35,560
And this is maximum pump speed of the flow,

701
00:41:35,640 --> 00:41:37,180
which is making a bit of a mess here.

702
00:41:37,460 --> 00:41:42,000
This pump, when it's implanted in the natural heart, is silent.

703
00:41:42,160 --> 00:41:44,300
The patients don't hear the pump.

704
00:41:44,500 --> 00:41:45,700
They don't feel the pump.

705
00:41:46,540 --> 00:41:47,960
And that's exactly what you need.

706
00:41:49,000 --> 00:41:54,200
In 2006, there were 180 Jarvik 2000 implantees.

707
00:41:55,360 --> 00:41:58,000
Peter Houghton was the first patient to receive one.

708
00:41:58,620 --> 00:42:00,460
It's been remarkably robust.

709
00:42:00,640 --> 00:42:02,520
I've had no troubles with it whatsoever.

710
00:42:02,800 --> 00:42:04,600
And that's been the surprising thing.

711
00:42:04,700 --> 00:42:06,900
I've had troubles with aging-related things,

712
00:42:07,000 --> 00:42:09,100
all of which got nothing whatever to do with the heart.

713
00:42:09,200 --> 00:42:12,840
The heart pumps away relentlessly, whether I like it or not.

714
00:42:15,980 --> 00:42:20,020
The Jarvik 2000 is approved for lifetime use in Europe.

715
00:42:20,120 --> 00:42:23,460
FDA approval in the United States is likely.

716
00:42:23,560 --> 00:42:26,260
It promises to provide an alternative for patients

717
00:42:26,360 --> 00:42:29,860
unable to receive a heart transplant.

718
00:42:29,861 --> 00:42:32,320
I think the technology of the Jarvik 2000

719
00:42:32,420 --> 00:42:35,100
is good enough to be widely applied at hundreds

720
00:42:35,200 --> 00:42:37,200
of medical centers throughout the country.

721
00:42:37,300 --> 00:42:42,200
In the long term, the Jarvik 2000 could probably benefit 50

722
00:42:42,300 --> 00:42:43,700
or 100,000 people a year.

723
00:42:47,220 --> 00:42:49,820
Over 2,000 years of pump technology

724
00:42:49,920 --> 00:42:53,320
have taken us from the Archimedean screw

725
00:42:53,420 --> 00:42:56,660
to the artificial heart.

726
00:42:56,661 --> 00:43:01,540
Pumps are the unsung heroes of industry and everyday life.

727
00:43:01,640 --> 00:43:06,040
And the pumps of tomorrow may very well add years to our lives.

